Introduction

	The syntax for Docker can be categorized into four main groups:
	
		○ Running a container
		○ Managing & Inspecting containers
		○ Managing Docker images
		○ Docker daemon stats and information
	
	
	

	Docker Images 
	
		○ Docker images are instructions for what a container should do, so we need to download a image and set it to the Docker container 
		
		○ Docker images has a image name and a tag, we have to specify both before downloading 
		
			§ because multiple images can have the same name but not the same tag 
			
			
		
		Commands:
		
			§ docker pull imagename:tagname   (to download a image to your device)  
			
			§ docker image (lists all commands relating to docker images) 
			
			§ docker image ls (lists all downloaded docker images) 
		
			§ docker image rm imagename:tagname (to delete a downloaded docker image)
			
			
	
	
Running a Docker container 

	• The run commands creates containers from images 
			
			he command works in the following way: docker run [OPTIONS] IMAGE_NAME [COMMAND] [ARGUMENTS...]  the options enclosed in brackets are not required for a container to run.
			
			
	
			
			
			
			
	• An image named "helloworld"
	
	• "Interactively" by providing the -it switch in the [OPTIONS] command. This will allow us to interact with the container directly.
	
	• I am going to spawn a shell within the container by providing /bin/bash as the [COMMAND] part. This argument is where you will place what commands you want to run within the container (such as a file, application or shell!)
	
	• So, to achieve the above, my command will look like the following: docker run -it helloworld /bin/bash
	
			
	
